# Appropriation Bill
Tamil title: நிதி ஒதுக்கீடு சட்ட முன்வடிவு

Speech 18, delivered on 7 April 1958.

**Mr C. N. Annadurai:** Honourable Speaker, for more than a month members on the government and opposition sides have expressed their views on the purposes and departments for which the money entrusted to the administration this year should be used. As this discussion draws to a close, I believe we have reached a clear position.

The merit of the government depends upon how it allocates this year’s funds among the various departments and puts them to proper use. More than that, the welfare or adversity of this country depends upon it. Yesterday the Finance Minister said that he had passed the age at which he needed to receive another person’s praise.

**Mr C. Subramaniam:** Aasai Thambi’s praise—

**Mr C. N. Annadurai:** He said he was no longer at an age when he sought that praise. I wish to point out that receiving Aasai Thambi’s praise together with other appreciation would not be a loss to the Finance Minister; it could only be a gain.

The rulers must firmly bear one fact in mind. Although parties differ from one another and members reject opposing views for that reason, representatives have set party considerations aside to some extent and insisted that their constituencies be properly attended to. Three kinds of complaint have been raised: constituencies are not properly looked after; adequate funds are not allocated; and allocated funds are not properly spent.

Members of the ruling party must recognise that their own members have identified administrative corruption and defects on an even larger scale than we on this side have. The great influence enjoyed by the ruling party today should bring it not only happiness but also grave responsibility and concern. If an opposition capable of easily defeating it existed, even its minor errors would not afflict the public for long. But because the opposition currently cannot overthrow the ruling party, its members must possess the generosity to examine carefully every view expressed from this side.

Our Finance Minister repeatedly says, both inside and outside the House, that no well-formed proposals come from the opposition. Our inability to provide such proposals may arise from lack of experience. Or perhaps the government’s dictionary, through long habit, defines every proposal we offer as lacking form. When opposition members speak about defects in their constituencies, the ruling party should treat those statements as the views of constituency representatives and examine them accordingly.

This State forms part of the Indian subcontinent. Its welfare or adversity, development or obstruction, consequently depends upon the subcontinent’s larger plans; our financial condition is affected by its finances. We must refer to that fact when we speak. The Finance Minister often complains that we therefore use the words “North” and “South” too frequently. He should not assume that the distinction is defective, or that using it to examine other questions corrupts their character.

During the budget debate, the question of Dravida Nadu and separation arose prominently. When I stated my view, the Finance Minister replied by examining antecedents: the history of our party and my own associations during that history. I take pride in the fact that our antecedents attracted his attention. If I began examining individual members of the Congress or the Congress Party’s entire ancestry, I have ample political material available. If the Finance Minister, despite his long experience, finds pleasure in stirring up such dirt, I shall not prevent him. But it is futile to imagine a connection between our request not to investigate antecedents and the demand for Dravida Nadu.

The claim that development is greater in the North and lower in the South is not made by our side alone. At this point the source reproduces a long English quotation, but its characters are almost completely corrupted in OCR and cannot be translated reliably from this text.

The Delhi Parliament was the place where the question was asked: should we remove one existing imperialism only to introduce another new imperialism? The person who raised it was the Honourable Ananthasayanam Ayyangar, now Speaker of Parliament, when he was an ordinary member. If a Congress member could ask such a question in Parliament, then even though he did not elaborate it to the same degree, his mind contained the same North-South concern that I express. I am obliged to point that out to this House. The short English sentence quoted immediately afterward is likewise unreadable in the OCR.

I wish to note that the debates conducted in this House are considered of a high standard and receive praise outside this State as well. Yet a debate preserves that standard only when a minister who rises to reply addresses the questions already raised. On a previous occasion the Finance Minister said, “Even if Mr Annadurai had not spoken about the separation of Dravida Nadu, I came prepared to discuss it,” and proceeded with his prepared speech. Instead, the ruling party must listen to the defects identified by the opposition and offer appropriate answers. Only then can a debate function well.

This is not merely my judgement or that of some great person on our side. I wish to place before the House the view of another distinguished figure whom the influential ruling party can accept. The source then prints a lengthy English quotation which OCR has rendered illegible.

The person who expressed that view was the late S. Satyamurti, acclaimed throughout Tamil Nadu and the Indian subcontinent for his parliamentary ability. The same principle he stated is relevant to what occurs here today. I therefore respectfully ask the Finance Minister, when concluding a debate, not to introduce a new issue but to follow the practice of answering what has already been said.

When we assess how the large sum entrusted to the government will be spent, we must remember another scholar’s view: a country’s condition cannot be judged merely by whether its budget has a deficit, surplus, or exact balance. Another brief English quotation setting out that proposition is unreadable in the OCR.

**Honourable C. Subramaniam:** If you say it, I am prepared to accept it.

**Mr C. N. Annadurai:** Our Honourable Finance Minister must certainly accept it, because this is the view of great thinkers.

Another long English passage follows in the source but is too severely corrupted to reconstruct. I stress its principle because the Finance Minister should accept the same view that he himself expressed while sitting in this House in an earlier capacity. In a budget, our objective must therefore not be confined to the amount of revenue received and money spent. We must seek to maximise the benefit produced by that spending.

The Finance Minister has asked that concrete proposals be placed before the House. I therefore wish to submit, with a small modification, a suggestion offered by the Honourable Mahalingam. He said that assigning too many departments to one minister imposes an excessive workload. To distribute that work, I suggest appointing a member of the Muslim community as a minister and assigning him one of the two or three departments now held by the Finance Minister.

Muslims form a significant community in this State and support the measures taken by the ruling party today. Even when a dispute concerns India and Kashmir, they have the courage and generosity to say, “We shall not support Kashmir; what the Indian Government does is just.” Some say that I make this proposal to cast a net and capture Muslim support. We know that the Congress Government possesses the larger net. I am not speaking in order to cast one. I insist that the Muslim community should receive proper representation in this ministry.

Because this State Government’s finances depend upon the Central Government’s actions, our ministry bears responsibility for responding properly to all measures taken by the Centre. I therefore offer another concrete proposal. The administration itself should purchase the handloom cloth lying unsold and thereby provide immediate relief to handloom weavers.

Our ministers have strongly condemned the Central Government’s reduction of the rebate. They must ensure that this strong condemnation does not become very gentle by the time it reaches Delhi. They should make every effort to prevent the rebate reduction and secure substantial benefit for handloom workers.

Our friend Mada Gowder argued very emotionally and angrily that this House should meet at Ootacamund; by the end, one might even say that tears and agitation entered his appeal. I am deeply sorry that, despite hearing such feeling, I cannot change my decision. I cannot accept his argument that holding the Assembly there would itself cause Ootacamund to develop.

I wholeheartedly support Mada Gowder’s desire that Ootacamund should develop. But I cannot accept that convening this House there would achieve it. He asked, “Do you refuse to come because you seek the welfare of Tamils? Are the people living in Ootacamund not Tamils?” No one on our side said that we would refuse to go there in the interests of Tamils.

If I remember correctly, the Honourable Mada Gowder once stated in this very House, “I am not a Tamil; I am a Kannadiga.” I mention this today not to decide who is or is not Tamil. Long ago the Tamils embraced the broad outlook, “Every place is our town; everyone is our kin.” The issue here is not whether Tamils will suffer or prosper. I simply disagree that the Assembly’s meeting at Ootacamund would produce development there.

Mada Gowder said regretfully that the proposal was being stopped because I did not accept it. I tell him this: the government has shown generosity in requiring the agreement of a particular party before undertaking such a matter. Let him extend that generosity a little further. Our particular party asks that this State be named Tamil Nadu. If he persuades the government and recommends that name, the DMK will consent to travelling to Ootacamund on the very next day after the State is named Tamil Nadu.

Do not imagine that I say this to bargain. I say it to demonstrate that even though you belong to Congress, you cannot change the rulers: if they do not favour even your demand, they will not accept it. I ask him to consider that political reality.

Holding the Assembly at Ootacamund will not by itself bring the town great benefits. Better proposals have been offered, and I welcome them with confidence. It was suggested that Ootacamund’s natural beauty and attractions be developed and that it be made an excellent place of residence. Such measures would genuinely develop it. I humbly ask honourable members not to turn Ootacamund’s development into a party question or cast aspersions upon others.

For ministers to learn the defects in each constituency directly, members should invite them to their constituencies. The ministers, accompanied by the representatives and district officials, should tour each constituency for four days. The Minister for Harijan Welfare should make particular arrangements for this. All the deficiencies of a constituency cannot be explained in a five-minute speech.

I therefore ask the ministers to act as I have suggested. I conclude by urging the government to spend well the large sums allocated in the budget and use them to benefit the people.

---NOTES---
The first scan clearly prints the title “நிதி ஒதுக்கீடு சட்ட முன்வடிவு,” although the OCR heading is almost unrecognisable. The verified title is used in English while the extracted Tamil source is preserved verbatim.

Images 4–6 contain several English quotations printed in Latin script. Tamil-language OCR transformed them into mixed Tamil numerals and Latin fragments, making exact recovery impossible from the OCR text. Their locations, speakers where recoverable, and surrounding arguments are retained; no quotation has been invented.

“உருவான யோசனை” is rendered contextually as a concrete or well-formed proposal. “துண்டு விழுகிற பட்ஜெட்” refers to a deficit budget.

The historical expression “Harijan Welfare” is retained as the official departmental name used in the period source.
